        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to act as an academic review planning editor to harmonize the scope and chapter proposals for a systematic review on \"How does the gut microbiome modulate the efficacy of immune checkpoint inhibitors in solid tumours, and what mechanisms mediate that effect?\"\n\nI need to:\n1. Reconcile chapter proposals before detailed expansion\n2. Set one shared scope, ordered outline, chapter boundaries, concepts, terminology, and transitions\n3. Remove overlap and resolve contradictions between proposals while preserving their broad useful source assignments\n4. Do not replace routed literature with only deep-read seeds\n5. Remove a routed source only when explicitly named in excluded_source_handles with a reason\n6. Merge and deduplicate supplement gaps and directed-read tasks by source_handle while preserving all distinct questions and chapter_ids\n7. Preserve valuable level-1 tool results and do not reacquire review-reported original full texts\n8. Return shared_outline, chapters (with chapter_id and coordinated scope), supplement_requests, directed_reads, and harmonization_notes\n\nKey issues to address based on the input:\n1. The shared_level1_outline already provides a coherent 5-chapter structure (CH01-CH05)\n2. Chapter proposals have been provided with detailed substantive threads\n3. Supplement requests show some gaps remain unmet (Phase III RCTs, inosine concentrations)\n4. Source routing has been completed with interpretation limits\n5. Need to ensure no overlap between chapters while maintaining logical flow\n\nKey harmonization tasks:\n1. Ensure CH01 (Clinical Evidence) doesn't overlap with CH04 (Interventions) on FMT data\n2. Ensure CH02 (Microbial Features) doesn't overlap with CH03 (Mechanisms) on metabolite discussions\n3. Ensure CH05 (Limitations) doesn't simply repeat gaps already stated in CH01\n4. Consolidate supplement requests to avoid duplication\n5. Ensure directed reads are properly assigned to chapters\n\nLet me structure the output:\n\n1. shared_outline: Maintain the 5-chapter structure but refine boundaries\n2. chapters: Coordinate scope for each chapter with clear boundaries\n3. supplement_requests: Merge duplicate requests, keep only unique gaps\n4. directed_reads: Assign to specific chapters based on content relevance\n5. harmonization_notes: Document key decisions on scope boundaries and source assignments\n\nKey decisions:\n- CH01 should focus on observational clinical evidence (antibiotics, PPIs) and state the Phase III RCT gap as confirmed fact\n- CH02 should focus on microbial taxa and metabolite associations without deep mechanism discussion\n- CH03 should focus on mechanistic pathways (immune, metabolic, barrier) with clear species distinction (mouse vs human)\n- CH04 should focus on intervention feasibility (FMT, probiotics, diet) with safety/standardization discussion\n- CH05 should synthesize limitations without repeating CH01's Phase III gap as a new discovery\n\nFor supplement requests:\n- Merge the Phase III RCT gap requests (case_E_phase3_rct appears multiple times)\n- Merge the inosine concentration gap requests (case_C2_inosine_tme appears multiple times)\n- Keep only unique, non-duplicated requests\n\nFor directed reads:\n- Assign P0575 (antibiotic timing) to CH01\n- Assign P0582 (inosine/UBA6) to CH02/CH03\n- Assign P0583 (FMT-LUMINate) to CH04\n- Ensure no duplication across chapters\n\nLet me create the harmonized output.",
